{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Identify the like terms (same letters, same powers)",
   "Add or subtract coefficients of like terms",
   "For multiplication: multiply coefficients, add powers",
   "For division: divide coefficients, subtract powers"
  ],
  "title": "How to Simplify Algebraic Expressions",
  "content": "<p><strong>Simplifying</strong> means collecting like terms and reducing an expression to its shortest form. <strong>Like terms</strong> have the same letter(s) raised to the same power — e.g. \\(3x\\) and \\(5x\\) are like terms; \\(3x\\) and \\(3x^2\\) are not.</p><p><strong>Collecting like terms:</strong> Add or subtract the coefficients of matching terms. Constants (numbers without letters) are also like terms with each other.</p><p><strong>Multiplying terms:</strong> Multiply the coefficients and add the powers of matching letters. E.g. \\(3x^2 \\times 4x^3 = 12x^5\\).</p><p><strong>Dividing terms:</strong> Divide the coefficients and subtract the powers. E.g. \\(12x^5 \\div 4x^2 = 3x^3\\).</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Simplify</strong> \\(5x + 3y - 2x + 7y\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Group like terms: \\(x\\)-terms: \\(5x - 2x\\); \\(y\\)-terms: \\(3y + 7y\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(5x - 2x = 3x\\) and \\(3y + 7y = 10y\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(3x + 10y\\)</p>"
 },
